tags.description:
A quick start for the server that receives Pixonaut's events: register an
endpoint, verify each request, and acknowledge it. The rules behind each step,
the retry schedule and every event's payload are under
[Webhooks](#tag/webhooks).

## 1. Register an endpoint

In the studio, under **Webhooks**, choose **Add endpoint** and give it the URL
your server receives on. Leave **Event types** unchecked to receive every
event, or check the ones you handle. Copy the endpoint's signing secret when
it is shown: it is shown once.

## 2. Verify each request

Read the raw body as it arrived, before parsing it, and check its signature
with the endpoint's secret. In Node:

```js
import crypto from "node:crypto";

const TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300;
const MS_PER_SECOND = 1000;

// `headers` with lowercase names, `rawBody` the bytes as they arrived.
export function verifyWebhook(secret, headers, rawBody, now = Date.now()) {
  const timestamp = Number(headers["x-pixonaut-timestamp"]);
  if (!Number.isInteger(timestamp)) return false;
  if (Math.abs(now / MS_PER_SECOND - timestamp) > TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
    return false;
  }
  const expected = crypto
    .createHmac("sha256", secret)
    .update(String(timestamp))
    .update(".")
    .update(rawBody)
    .digest();
  // The header may carry other schemes beside this one, comma-separated.
  return String(headers["x-pixonaut-signature"] ?? "")
    .split(",")
    .some((entry) => {
      const [scheme, hex = ""] = entry.trim().split("=");
      if (scheme !== "v1" || !/^[0-9a-f]+$/.test(hex)) return false;
      const given = Buffer.from(hex, "hex");
      return (
        given.length === expected.length &&
        crypto.timingSafeEqual(given, expected)
      );
    });
}
```

Refuse a request that does not verify, without acting on its body.

## 3. Store, then acknowledge

A delivery answered with a `2xx` is never sent again, so answer one only
once the event is safe: either handle it before answering, or store or
enqueue the verified event durably and answer `200` then, handling it from
there. A slow or failed answer is retried, and a retry carries the same
event `id`, so skip an id whose work has succeeded. Count an id as handled
only once its work has, so that work which failed is done again when the
event is retried or picked up from your queue:

<!-- example: webhook print.completed -->
```json
{
  "id": "1790000090000-3b4c5d6e-7f8a-4b9c-8d0e-1f2a3b4c5d6e",
  "type": "print.completed",
  "createdAt": 1790000090000,
  "payload": { "printId": "c4d5e6f7-a8b9-4c0d-9e1f-2a3b4c5d6e7f" }
}
```

## 4. Follow what a store sells

A template a store sells can be paused by a member, after which customers
cannot design on it. Each pause and each resume is one `template.live_changed`,
naming the template by `baseId`: `live: false` is a pause, `live: true` a
resume. Hide the designer for a paused template and show it again once it is
resumed. A delivery can be missed, and a store connected after a pause heard
nothing of it, so the event is a prompt rather than the record: `status` on
`GET /v1/templates/{id}` answers `live` or `paused` for any version of the
template, and is the state to trust.

A store that keeps the address of a link it handed out, such as a designer
link on a product page, follows `share_link.revoked` and
`share_link.regenerated`: the address it kept no longer opens. Neither
carries the new address; a designer link's is read again from the share-link
list, as its `url`. A connected store's endpoint hears of the links its own
keys handed out; an endpoint no store owns hears of every one.

Use **Send test request** in the endpoint's delivery log to check that your
server is reachable and verifies; the test request is signed the same way.
